Title: A CHRISTMAS CAROL

Credit: Written by  
Author: Charles Dickens  
Source: Adapted from book  
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING HOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE - DAY

A cold, bleak office. EBENEZER SCROOGE, a tight-fisted, grumpy old man, sits at his desk, counting money. The room is dimly lit, with a small fire barely warming the space.

BOB CRATCHIT, Scrooge's underpaid clerk, shivers in a small cell-like office nearby, trying to warm himself by a tiny candle.

SCROOGE
(looking up)
Bah! Humbug!

FRED, Scrooge's cheerful nephew, bursts in, full of holiday spirit.

FRED
A merry Christmas, Uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug! What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
Come, Uncle! What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
(angrily)
Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine!

FRED
But you don't keep it!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
And a Happy New Year!

FRED exits, shaking his head. Scrooge returns to his work.

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS EVE - NIGHT

The streets are bustling with people preparing for Christmas. Scrooge walks home, grumbling as he passes by cheerful families.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge enters his dark, dreary room. He locks the door and prepares for bed. Suddenly, he sees MARLEY'S GHOST, wrapped in chains, appear before him.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Ebenezer Scrooge!

SCROOGE
(startled)
What do you want with me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I wear the chain I forged in life. You will be haunted by three spirits.

SCROOGE
(terrified)
Is that the chance and hope you mentioned?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Expect the first tomorrow when the bell tolls one.

The ghost fades away, leaving Scrooge trembling.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge awakens to the sound of the clock striking one. The first spirit, the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST, appears, looking like a child and an old man at once.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Come! Walk with me!

They fly through the window, landing in a snowy field where young Scrooge is seen at school, lonely and forgotten.

YOUNG SCROOGE
(sadly)
I wish I could go home.

The spirit leads Scrooge to a scene where he sees himself with his first love, BELLE.

BELLE
You have changed, Ebenezer. You love money more than me.

Scrooge watches, heartbroken, as she leaves him.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge wakes again, this time to the sound of the clock striking two. The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT, a jolly giant, appears.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in and know me better, man!

They walk through the streets, witnessing the joy of Christmas. They visit the CRATCHIT household, where BOB CRATCHIT and his family celebrate despite their poverty.

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM, a frail boy, smiles brightly.

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

Scrooge feels warmth in his heart.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge wakes to the sound of the clock striking three. The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME, a dark, hooded figure, appears.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME
(points silently)

Scrooge follows the ghost to a graveyard, where he sees a group of businessmen discussing the death of a man.

BUSINESSMAN 1
Who cares? He was a miser.

They point to a grave, and Scrooge approaches it, trembling.

SCROOGE
(terrified)
No! Please, tell me this isn't my grave!

He looks at the tombstone and sees his name: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
(pleading)
I can change! I will honor Christmas in my heart!

The ghost's hand trembles.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - MORNING

Scrooge wakes up in his own bed, filled with joy.

SCROOGE
I’m alive! It’s Christmas Day!

He rushes to the window and opens it.

SCROOGE
(to a passing boy)
You there! What day is it?

BOY
Christmas Day, sir!

SCROOGE
Go buy the biggest turkey you can find and bring it here!

EXT. LONDON STREET - DAY

Scrooge walks through the streets, greeting everyone with a smile.

SCROOGE
Merry Christmas! Happy New Year!

He arrives at the CRATCHIT household with the turkey.

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY

The Cratchit family is surprised and overjoyed to see Scrooge.

BOB CRATCHIT
Mr. Scrooge!

SCROOGE
I’ve come to celebrate Christmas with you!

TINY TIM
(cheerfully)
God bless us, every one!

FADE OUT.
